WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:10.250
Every number in a research report has a history: the file it came from, the code that touched it, and the person who ran that code.

2
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.750
R is built for keeping that history honest.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.450
It is free, scriptable, and every step of an analysis can be written down and run again.

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.350
Let's learn the core of R the way research programmers actually use it.

5
00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.110
Everything in R starts with the vector: an ordered collection of values of one type.

6
00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:28.200
Create one with c().

7
00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.540
Here are six site codes from a study export.

8
00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.480
Ask R for the type with typeof(), and the size with length().

9
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.440
Most R functions are vectorized, so toupper() changes every element at once.

10
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.640
No loop required.

11
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.890
Because a vector holds one type, mixing types forces a conversion.

12
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.310
Combine the number one with the text two, and both become text.

13
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:54.250
This silent coercion is a classic source of bugs: one stray character in a numeric column turns the whole column into text.

14
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.150
Check the types whenever data arrives.

15
00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.180
When you repeat a step, turn it into a function.

16
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:09.840
clean_visit() takes a vector of raw visit labels, trims stray spaces, converts to lower case, and rewrites each spelling to the word Week, followed by the number, using sub(), a pattern replacement.

17
00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.720
The last value computed is what the function returns.

18
00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.280
Now one tested function cleans every label, in every file.

19
00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.500
Real data has gaps.

20
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:24.690
R marks a missing value as NA, and NA is contagious: the mean of a vector containing NA is itself NA.

21
00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.220
That is deliberate.

22
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.110
R refuses to guess.

23
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:34.820
Find the gaps using the function is.na(), count them with sum(), and drop them only on purpose, by setting na.rm = TRUE.

24
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.940
A data frame is a table built from vectors.

25
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.360
Each column is a vector, and every column has the same length.

26
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.480
Our synthetic trial has an id, a treatment arm, a visit, and an outcome.

27
00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:53.280
Pull out a column with $, keep rows with a logical condition, and you are already asking real questions of the data.

28
00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.020
Most reports summarise by group.

29
00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:05.250
The dplyr package gives you a readable vocabulary: filter() keeps the Week 12 visit, group_by() splits by arm, and summarise() computes each statistic.

30
00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:06.850
Watch the missing outcome.

31
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:11.540
n() counts rows, not observations, so count the non-missing values explicitly.

32
00:02:11.830 --> 00:02:14.180
Precise definitions make a table trustworthy.

33
00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:17.490
Now a realistic case.

34
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.670
A collaborator sends an enrollment export.

35
00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:26.490
Site codes are inconsistent, some ages are blank, one participant appears twice, and the enrollment date is written three different ways.

36
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.230
Read every column as text first, so nothing is silently converted.

37
00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:32.870
Then fix each problem with a small, named step.

38
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:35.660
Dates need care.

39
00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:40.240
Parse the ISO format first, then fill the remaining gaps with the other formats, one at a time.

40
00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:47.420
Turn the arm column into a factor with its levels stated explicitly, remove exact duplicate rows with unique(), and sort by participant.

41
00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:52.600
Finally, check the result: one row per participant, and no unexpected missing values.

42
00:02:54.500 --> 00:03:00.570
That is the Data Lab toolkit: vectors, functions, missing values, and data frames, applied to messy research data.

43
00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:02.210
Your first mission is waiting.

44
00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:08.150
Write clean_site(), a new function built from the same steps, and the validator will test it on codes you have never seen.

45
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:10.550
Pass every check to earn your first XP.
